<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baška tablet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baška tablet is a stone slab from the church of St Lucy at Jurandvor near Baška, on the island of Krk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, dated to around 1100. Its inscription in Glagolitic script records King Zvonimir's gift of land to the Benedictine abbey there, and it is one of the oldest and most important monuments of the Croatian language and literacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baska-tablet.jpg" alt="Baška tablet" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hrvatska enciklopedija · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ba%C5%A1ka_tablet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tablet is made of white limestone. It is 199 cm wide, 99.5 cm high and 7.5 to 9 cm thick, and weighs about 800 kilograms. It stood as the left panel of the screen between the altar and the rest of the church. Historical sources say the screen survived until 1751, and it was probably destroyed some time between then and 1851. Scholars date the inscription to the late 11th or early 12th century. The abbot Držiha speaks of King Zvonimir's gift as a past event, which suggests it was cut after the king's death in 1089.</p><p>Not all agree. The art historian Pavuša Vežić argued for a church of the early 14th century and a text of about 1300, but scholars generally reject a date later than the early 12th century because the language and spelling are too archaic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The inscription records King Zvonimir's donation of a piece of land to a Benedictine abbey in the time of abbot Držiha. Its second half tells how abbot Dobrovit built the church with nine monks. It is written in Glagolitic in the Croatian recension of Church Slavonic, with traits of the Chakavian dialect, and it mixes in some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Cyrillic letters. It is the only example of the shift from the rounded Glagolitic type to the angular Croatian type.</p><p>The meaning of its opening letters is contested. Some read them as the word for I, others as a coded year, and dates of 1077, 1079, 1100, 1105 and 1120 have been proposed. One mention, of Mikula in Otočac, is also disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Petar Dorčić found the tablet on 15 September 1851 while the pavement of the church was being laid, and a small part was already broken. Since 1934 the original has been kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-academy-of-sciences-and-arts">Croatian Academy of Sciences and Arts</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, and a replica stands in the church. The archaeologist Branko Fučić's reconstruction of the text is the most widely accepted. The tablet names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and King Zvonimir for the first time in Croatian. It appeared on the Croatian 100 kuna banknote and on a postage stamp issued in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baška tablet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Baška tablet</b> records Zvonimir's gift in Glagolitic script.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li></ul></section>
